;; sandbox_verus.sb — Seatbelt profile TEMPLATE for the S2-Rust referee (amendment 16, the VeruSAGE stage-0
;; regime boundary). `check_verus.py` renders __EXEC_ALLOW__, __WRITE_PATHS__ and __DENY_READ__ and passes the
;; text to `/usr/bin/sandbox-exec -p` around every Verus run on agent-influenced text.
;; Seatbelt is LAST-MATCH-WINS: the allows must come AFTER the denies.
;;
;; ⛔⛔ THIS PROFILE IS DELIBERATELY WIDER THAN `s2lean/sandbox_check.sb`, IN EXACTLY ONE RESPECT, AND THE
;; DIFFERENCE IS FORCED BY THE TOOL RATHER THAN CHOSEN. S2-Lean denies `process-fork` outright: `lean` is a
;; single process and must never spawn, so refuter R1's `IO.Process.run` chain dies at the fork. VERUS CANNOT
;; RUN UNDER THAT RULE — it is a driver, not a verifier. Measured, red-first, on a real reference file:
;;
;;     (deny process-fork)  =>  "verus: rustup not found, or not executable"  then a panic at
;;                              rust_verify/src/lifetime.rs:333 "could not execute lifetime rustc process", rc 1
;;
;; So the fork is ALLOWED and the whole weight of the exec fence rests on `process-exec*` instead: a forked
;; child may exist, but it may only ever become one of the FOUR pinned binaries below. Nothing else on the
;; machine is reachable from inside this profile — proven by a positive control, not asserted.
;;
;; ⭐ THE EXEC SET IS FOUR BINARIES, AND "VERUS EXECS ONLY z3" (amendment 15 §8, open question (ii)) IS FALSE.
;; The `verus` binary is a SHIM: it resolves the pinned Rust toolchain by running `rustup`, which execs
;; `rust_verify`, which execs `z3`. Established by a four-step ladder in which each arm's own error names the
;; next binary in the chain — i.e. every member is proven NECESSARY by its own red arm:
;;
;;     allow {verus}                          -> error: failed to execute rust_verify (PermissionDenied)
;;     allow {verus,rustup}                   -> error: command failed: '.../rust_verify': Operation not permitted
;;     allow {verus,rustup,rust_verify}       -> error: could not execute z3 process (Operation not permitted)
;;     allow {verus,rustup,rust_verify,z3}    -> verification results:: 1 verified, 0 errors
;;
;; ⛔ AND THE SET IS COMPLETE ONLY BECAUSE IT WAS MEASURED OVER A POPULATION, NOT OVER A FIXTURE. My first
;; green arm ran a 1.3 kB PROOF-ONLY file, which never reaches Verus's lifetime/borrow driver at all; the
;; existence of a fifth exec point (`lifetime rustc`) was revealed by the RED arm's error text, not by the
;; green one. The completeness claim rests on the fence DIFFERENTIAL: 61 reference files drawn seeded
;; (seed 20260902) from the drawable population AC u NR, each run FENCED and UNFENCED, verdicts compared —
;; **61/61 agree, 0 differ**. A fifth binary would have turned some fenced run into COMPILE.
;;   ⇒ A WHITELIST IS COMPLETE ONLY OVER THE SHAPES IT WAS MEASURED ON, and a green arm on one fixture
;;     measures the fixture. The red arm reached further into the program than the green arm did.
;;
;; ⛔ `rustup` IS THE ONE MEMBER THAT IS NOT PART OF THE PINNED VERUS RELEASE. It lives on the user's PATH
;; (~/.cargo/bin/rustup), is user-writable, and is therefore pinned BY SHA in HASHES-S2RUST.txt alongside the
;; release's own three, with the toolchain channel recorded beside it. A binary admitted to a fence must be
;; pinned by the same instrument that pins the ones it stands next to.
;;
;; What each leg fences, every one of them driven by a positive control on this exact profile text (the
;; control adds /bin/sh + /bin/bash to the exec list, and nothing else, so a probe can exercise the legs
;; Verus itself never touches — the one difference is declared, never silent):
;;   network*      — no sockets. Probe: bash /dev/tcp connect => "Operation not permitted".
;;   process-exec* — only the four pinned binaries. Probe: /usr/bin/whoami => denied.
;;   file-write*   — denied everywhere except the fresh per-check work dir and /dev/null, so a body cannot
;;                   overwrite bodies.json or reach the state root. Probe: `: > ~/FENCE_ESCAPE_PROBE` =>
;;                   denied AND no such file on disk afterwards; `: > <work>/x` => allowed.
;;   file-read*    — DENIED for the credential/config trees and for the run's own state root (__DENY_READ__,
;;                   DERIVED from $BENCH — row CO: a fence that NAMES a path stops protecting the day the work
;;                   moves). Probe: read ~/.ssh/known_hosts => denied, while a non-denied path stays readable,
;;                   so the leg is proven to bite WITHOUT being blanket.
(version 1)
(allow default)
(deny network*)
(deny process-exec*)
__DENY_READ__
(allow process-exec __EXEC_ALLOW__)
(deny file-write*)
(allow file-write* __WRITE_PATHS__ (literal "/dev/null"))
